WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
It was hard, because I'd lived, you know, New Orleans, then Houston, then Mobile, and then here.

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.560
So you're dragging around these seventy or eighty boxes and eighty paintings.

3
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.620
And you really can't think.

4
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.040
One day I was in a drugstore in New Orleans getting prescriptions, and this woman said, "Don't move.

5
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.760
I want to show you something."

6
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:27.060
In the Times-Picayune, on the Living section, the whole page was devoted to what we were feeling.

7
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.000
Because I thought I was losing my mind.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.180
But there it was, she must have seen that look.

9
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.660
It says we have short term memory loss, we can't concentrate, we're depressed.

10
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:40.500
I thought, "Oh, that's me."

11
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.280
I can jump off of a bridge and nobody will care.

12
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.320
Because every symptom that was there, and it still exists,

13
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:53.440
because I still talk to friends in New Orleans, and they're miserable, because their life has changed.

14
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.680
Basically, your life was taken away from you.

15
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:03.020
Everything you knew, every place you ate, every friend you had, every connection you had,

16
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:10.020
every physician you had for thirty . . . It was all gone and you just couldn't get your act together.

17
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.400
So I kept that.

18
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:19.380
I don't know where it is, but it described how pitiful, even people who lost their homes, that was worse.

19
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.800
But the main issue of the article was you lost your life.
